o \"100% reliable\". So candidate C has strong reliability, calmness, technical, quick decisions, positive crew atmosphere, but communication difficulty, egocentric, unwilling to further education.\n\nCandidate D:\n- Responds to unexpected events adequately (decision under pressure)\n- Can concentrate very well (cognitive)\n- Solves problems extremely well (technical)\n- Takes responsibility seriously (reliability)\n- Arrogant (teamwork weakness)\n- Relatively weak leadership skills (teamwork)\n- Moody, loner (teamwork)\nNo explicit reliability beyond responsibility, no mention of calmness under pressure beyond adequate response.\n\nWe need to weigh strengths vs weaknesses for long-distance flights. Long-distance requires high reliability, good decision-making, calm under stress, ability to handle fatigue, teamwork, communication with crew, professional development.\n\nCandidate C seems strong overall: reliability 100%, quick correct decisions, calm in crisis, technical competence, positive crew atmosphere. Weaknesses: difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, unwilling to further education. The positive crew atmosphere may offset communication difficulty? But we must not mitigate weaknesses beyond data. However we can note that positive atmosphere is a strength.\n\nCandidate A has strong anticipation, complex connections, spatial vision, leadership, but unfriendly, unorganized, poor at criticism. Those could affect crew coordination on long flights.\n\nCandidate B has conscientiousness, stress handling, weather assessment, but poor communication and attitude.\n\nCandidate D has problem solving, concentration, responsibility, but poor leadership, moody, loner, arrogant.\n\nThus candidate C appears most balanced: high reliability, quick correct decisions, calm, positive crew atmosphere, technical competence. Weaknesses are communication difficulty and egocentric, but positive atmosphere suggests some teamwork ability. However we must not infer beyond explicit. The positive atmosphere is explicit, so it's a teamwork strength. The communication difficulty is explicit. So we have both.\n\nOverall, candidate C seems best despite communication issues because reliability and decision-making are critical. The other candidates have more severe interpersonal deficits (unfriendly, uncooperative, arrogant, loner) and also lack some technical/decision strengths.\n\nThus provisional preference: Candidate C.\n\nUncertainties: How severe are communication difficulties? No data on severity. Also unwillingness to further education could affect long-term suitability. But long-distance pilot may need ongoing training. That is a concern. However other candidates also have issues (A unorganized, D weak leadership). So still C.\n\nWhat would change vote: If we learned that candidate C's communication issues lead to critical miscommunication, or that his egocentric attitude undermines crew, or that his unwillingness to further education is a major barrier, we might reconsider.\n\nNow produce output."}
